# Single findings the secret scan skips, as gitleaks fingerprints:
# commit:file:rule:line. A fingerprint names its commit, so each line skips one
# finding in one commit and nothing else. The same value in another file, or
# added again by another commit, is still reported. The rule-wide exemptions
# live in .gitleaks.toml.
#
# test/fixtures/subtasks/keys.json, from f215453 ("Running subagents show as
# rows on their parent's card"): the "key" of agents a, c and cx. Each is the
# first 24 hex digits of
#   sha256(base + "\n" + source + "\n" + session_id + "\n" + raw agent_id)
# of public test values beside it in the same file, and test/subtasks.test.ts
# recomputes all five ("every agent key is the hash the hook computes"). The
# other two score under generic-api-key's entropy floor.
#
# Not every scan reads f215453. CI's, gitleaks-action, reads only a pull
# request's first 30 commits, which hold it, and the last 20 of each push, so
# the release push to master never reaches it. What reads it, and every other
# commit the release brings, is a scan of the whole range before that push:
#   gitleaks detect --log-opts="--no-merges --first-parent origin/master..HEAD"
# Master takes these commits as they are (it fast-forwards to the release), so
# these fingerprints still match for any scan that reads f215453. A rebase or
# cherry-pick gives the commit a new hash, and a scan that reads it reports
# the three values again rather than passing them.
f215453f1ace26b25bc30f0539dc278e737ae327:test/fixtures/subtasks/keys.json:generic-api-key:9
